            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Joseph Haydn's Early Life</strong><br>\nJoseph Haydn was born in Rohrau, Austria, in 1732. He was immersed in music from a young age, as his family was very musical. At the age of eight, Haydn was sent to Hainburg to receive formal musical training under the care of his relative, Johann Matthias Franck. Despite the hardships of his early years, which included struggling for food and a place to sleep, Haydn's exceptional musical talent shone through. He became a choirboy at St. Stephen's Cathedral in Vienna, where he gained a foundational education in music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Becoming a Professional Musician</strong><br>\nAfter his voice broke, and he could no longer sing in the choir, Haydn began his journey as a professional musician. He worked various jobs, teaching music lessons, playing for street serenades, and eventually became a valet-accompanist for the Italian composer Nicola Porpora. During this period, Haydn's exposure to new musical styles and his diligent study of counterpoint and composition laid the groundwork for his later work as a composer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Esterházy Patronage</strong><br>\nIn 1761, Haydn entered the most stable and productive phase of his career when he was appointed Kapellmeister to the wealthy Esterházy family. Haydn served the Esterházy court for nearly 30 years. During this time, he composed a vast array of music including symphonies, string quartets, and operas. His position provided him with financial security and the opportunity to experiment with musical form and expression within the comfort of an exclusive audience.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
