      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Developing the Symphony and String Quartet</strong><br>\nHaydn played a critical role in the development of the symphony and string quartet. He took these forms, which were still evolving during his early years, and expanded them, experimenting with structure and instrumentation. His works in these genres set the standards for composers who followed. Haydn's symphonies are known for their clear form, memorable melodies, and inventive use of themes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Haydn's Later Years and London Symphonies</strong><br>\nIn the late 1790s, after the death of Prince Nikolaus Esterházy, Haydn spent two successful periods in London, producing some of his most famous works, the \"London Symphonies.\" These symphonies were more grandiose and expressive than his earlier works and reflected Haydn's response to the vibrant musical scene in London. The experience enriched his style and increased his international fame.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Creation and The Seasons</strong><br>\nHaydn's oratorios \"The Creation\" and \"The Seasons\" are considered his masterpieces, combining his mature musical language with large-scale choral and orchestral forces. These works, inspired by Handel's oratorios, which Haydn had heard in London, showcased his ability to evoke powerful imagery and emotion through music. The oratorios cemented Haydn's legacy as a key figure of the Classical period.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
